# Conversion note: Eggs mandatory in list but method says if non-vegan; cabbage is one suggested vegetable. Preserve source-dependent choices without inferring an unconditional dietary label.

::ingredients

(tofu, extra firm) 1 block
(egg, conditional on non-vegan version in method) 3
(hard vegetables, optional; your choice; examples sugar snap peas / sliced carrots / broccoli / cauliflower) as desired
(cabbage, chopped, optional)
(sauce) = (soy sauce) as needed -OR- (teriyaki sauce) as needed
(garlic, minced) 2tbsp
(coconut oil) enough for coating wok and ~1/2in at bottom

::equipment

(wok, copper)
(pan, smaller)
(plate)

::instructions

Optional [
    (tofu) <freeze, at least 24h prior>
        <thaw>
]
(tofu) <cut, into cubes or slabs as preferred>
    <drain, carefully>
    <soak, in (sauce), a few minutes>
(coconut oil) <pour, into (wok), evenly coat the basin; ~1/2in at the bottom>
    <heat, medium heat>
{stir fry} = (coconut oil) + (tofu) + (sauce) + (garlic)
    <heat, in (wok), gradually increasing the heat> ?= tofu surface starts browning
Meanwhile [
    (tofu) <shift>
        <flip>
]
Optional [
    {stir fry} + (hard vegetables)
        <cook> ?= vegetables softened
]
If making the non-vegan version:
    (egg) <scramble fry, in (pan)> ?= no longer runny
    {stir fry} + (egg)
        <cook> ?= eggs firm or brown to your liking
Optional [
    {stir fry} + (cabbage)
        <cook> ?= cabbage softened while retaining some crunch
]
(wok) <remove, from the heat>
{stir fry} <serve, on (plate), with your favourite sides>

::notes

The source recommends optional freezing and thawing to make tofu spongier and chewier and aid flavour absorption.
Adding sauce and garlic early helps tofu absorb the flavours.
Save cabbage for last: it softens fast, so this lets it retain some crunch.

::story

A vegetarian dish that is easy to make and very flavorful. Goes well with rice or noodles

Serves 3 to 5.

Cooking time: ~10 to 20 minutes

